[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Assembly Loading, Reflection, and Metadata Inspection in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic programming capabilities in .NET empower developers to inspect assemblies, types, and members at runtime. This comprehensive guide covers Assembly Loading, Reflection, and Metadata Inspection, providing best practices for robust enterprise applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Assemblies and Dynamic Loading"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An assembly forms the fundamental building block of a .NET application, containing compiled Intermediate Language (IL) and metadata. Using the Assembly class, developers can load assemblies into memory dynamically at runtime."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Reflection;

class Program {
    static void Main() {
        // Load an assembly by its display name
        Assembly assembly = Assembly.Load("System.Text.Json");
        Console.WriteLine("Loaded Assembly: " + assembly.FullName);
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Assembly.Load: Loads an assembly given its long name or strong name."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Assembly.LoadFrom: Loads an assembly from a specific file path on disk."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Assembly.LoadFile: Loads an assembly from a path without resolving dependencies automatically."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Metadata Inspection via Reflection"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection is the process of examining metadata, types, properties, methods, and attributes of an assembly at runtime. The Type class serves as the primary gateway to reflection operations."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Reflection;

class Program {
    static void Main() {
        Type stringType = typeof(string);
        Console.WriteLine("Full Name: " + stringType.FullName);

        MethodInfo[] methods = stringType.GetMethods(BindingFlags.Public | BindingFlags.Instance);
        foreach (var method in methods) {
            Console.WriteLine("Method: " + method.Name);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Instantiating Types and Invoking Members Dynamically"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once a Type is obtained via reflection, you can instantiate objects, set property values, and invoke methods dynamically using Activator or MethodInfo.Invoke."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Reflection;

public class Calculator {
    public int Add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

class Program {
    static void Main() {
        Type calcType = typeof(Calculator);
        object calcInstance = Activator.CreateInstance(calcType);

        MethodInfo addMethod = calcType.GetMethod("Add");
        object[] parameters = new object[] { 10, 20 };
        object result = addMethod.Invoke(calcInstance, parameters);

        Console.WriteLine("Result of dynamic invocation: " + result);
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Performance Considerations and Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"While reflection and dynamic loading offer immense flexibility, they come with performance overhead due to runtime type checking and metadata parsing. Always cache Type objects and method references when performing frequent operations, and consider using source generators in modern .NET for high-performance scenarios."}]
